ewedAt":{"18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q5":"2026-08-01","18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q6":"2026-08-18","18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q7":"2026-08-18","18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q1":"2026-08-01","18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q7":"2026-10-02","18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q5":"2026-08-10","18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q1":"2026-08-12","18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q6":"2026-08-23"},"lang":"en","body":{"sections":[{"title":"Measure first — reading the execution plan","items":[{"id":"18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q1","q":"What is the standard process for investigating a slow query, and where should you start?","a":"The [[r:number-one mistake]] is jumping straight to `ADD INDEX`. Redundant indexes slow every write and consume the buffer pool.\n\n**Six steps:**\n\n1. **Identify the actual culprit** — use `pg_stat_statements`, the slow query log, or APM. Sort by [[o:total_time]] = mean × calls, not by mean alone. A 20 ms query called three million times hurts more than a 2 s query called ten times.\n2. **Reproduce it** — run against data whose distribution resembles production. A local table with 1,000 rows is always fast and proves nothing.\n3. **Read the plan** — use `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` on Postgres or `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` on MySQL 8. Find the node consuming the most time, not the first node displayed.\n4. **Form a hypothesis** — is an index missing? Is the predicate non-sargable? Is the join order poor? Are statistics inaccurate? Are unnecessary columns being read?\n5. **Change one thing and measure again** — changing two things at once makes it impossible to know which one helped.\n6. **Capture a baseline** — retain the good plan so it can be compared when a regression is suspected later.\n\n:::tip The question to ask first\n*'Does this query need to run at all?'* The fastest query is one that never runs: serve from cache, read from a read model, or eliminate a redundant call in the layer above.\n:::\n\n:::deep\nBefore optimizing, [[g:define the target]]: what p99 latency is required, and at what concurrency? Without a number, optimization has no stopping condition.\n\n- measure [[o:p95/p99]], not the average; averages hide exactly the tail latency users experience\n- `EXPLAIN` without ANALYZE only *estimates* and does not execute the statement; `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` executes it, so [[r:be careful with UPDATE/DELETE]] and wrap the statement in a transaction followed by `ROLLBACK`\n- the database cache makes a second run inherently faster; comparisons need the same cache state, and `BUFFERS` reveals whether pages came from disk or memory\n- on MySQL, use `SET profiling=1` or performance_schema; on Postgres, use `auto_explain` to log slow-query plans directly in production\n- 80% of production cases end with one of four causes: a missing appropriate index, `SELECT *`, an ORM N+1, or a large OFFSET\n\n**A safe PostgreSQL investigation ladder:**\n<pre><code>-- 1. No execution: capture a machine-readable candidate plan first\nEXPLAIN (COSTS, VERBOSE, FORMAT JSON)\nSELECT * FROM booking WHERE user_id=42 ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 50;\n-- 2. Execute only when the load and statement are safe\nBEGIN;\nSET LOCAL statement_timeout='3s';\nEXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, WAL, FORMAT JSON)\nSELECT * FROM booking WHERE user_id=42 ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 50;\nROLLBACK;\n-- compare plan node, estimated/actual rows, loops, shared hit/read and total time</code></pre>\n\n`ANALYZE` really executes the query; `ROLLBACK` does not make dangerous production work cheap, and DML may still lock rows or run triggers. Prefer production-shaped staging or a carefully bounded read.\n\n- **Benefit:** the before/after JSON plan turns tuning into evidence.\n- **Cost:** warm cache can hide I/O and one parameter can hide skew; preserve representative binds and repeat under controlled cache/load conditions.\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q2","q":"How do you read EXPLAIN ANALYZE in depth, and what do estimated versus actual rows, loops, and buffers reveal?","a":"An execution plan is a tree. Read it from the [[o:leaf nodes toward the root]], remembering that each node includes the *cumulative* cost of its subtree.\n\n<pre><code>Nested Loop  (cost=0.86..8534 rows=<span class=\"n\">1</span> width=64)\n             (actual time=0.05..<span class=\"r\">2841</span> rows=<span class=\"r\">48210</span> loops=<span class=\"n\">1</span>)\n  -&gt; Seq Scan on bookings b  (actual rows=<span class=\"n\">48210</span> loops=<span class=\"n\">1</span>)\n       Filter: (created_at &gt; now() - <span class=\"s\">'7 days'</span>)\n       Buffers: shared read=<span class=\"r\">31204</span>\n  -&gt; Index Scan on passengers p  (actual rows=<span class=\"n\">1</span> loops=<span class=\"r\">48210</span>)\n       Index Cond: (p.booking_id = b.id)</code></pre>\n\n**Three numbers demand attention:**\n\n- [[b:estimated rows versus actual rows]] — an estimate of 1 row versus 48,210 actual rows is a [[r:red flag]]. The optimiser chose a Nested Loop because it expected one row, but actually repeated the inner operation 48,210 times. The cause is almost always [[o:stale statistics or correlated columns]].\n- [[b:loops]] — a node's real cost is `actual time × loops`. A node showing 0.05 ms with loops=48210 consumes about 2.4 s. [[r:This is easy to overlook]].\n- [[b:Buffers]] — `shared hit` means a page came from memory and is cheap; `shared read` means it came from disk and is expensive. Reading 31,204 blocks is approximately 244 MB of I/O for one query.\n\n**Fix:** run `ANALYZE bookings;` to refresh statistics so the optimiser estimates cardinality correctly and can switch to a Hash Join.\n\n:::deep\n- in Postgres, `rows` is the row count [[o:per loop]]; MySQL `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` uses the same convention. The real total is rows × loops.\n- a large `Rows Removed by Filter` means many rows were read and discarded, so the predicate should be pushed into an index\n- high `Heap Fetches` during an Index Only Scan indicates a stale visibility map; run `VACUUM` to recover a true index-only scan\n- cardinality estimates can be wrong because of [[o:column correlation]] (`city='Hà Nội' AND district='Cầu Giấy'`—the optimiser multiplies probabilities as if they were independent): create `CREATE STATISTICS` (Postgres 10+) for the column pair\n- `Sort Method: external merge Disk: 84MB` means `work_mem` is insufficient and sorting has spilled to disk; increase work_mem for the session or eliminate unnecessary sorting\n- on MySQL, `Using filesort` and `Using temporary` together in a query whose `GROUP BY` and `ORDER BY` use different columns is a classic slow pattern\n- compare old and new plans with `explain.dalibo.com` or `pev2`; a visual tree is faster to inspect than raw text\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q3","q":"How does the optimiser choose join algorithms and join order, and when does it choose incorrectly?","a":"<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>How it runs</th><th>Cost</th><th>Best when</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Nested Loop</td><td>for each outer-table row → probe an index on the inner table</td><td>O(n × log m)</td><td>the outer table is <b>very small</b> and the inner table is indexed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hash Join</td><td>build a hash table from the smaller input and probe it with the larger input</td><td>O(n + m)</td><td>large equality joins with enough <code>work_mem</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Merge Join</td><td>sort both inputs and merge them</td><td>O(n log n + m log m)</td><td>both inputs are <b>already ordered</b> by the join key</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nThe optimiser is [[g:cost-based]]: it estimates each node's cardinality from statistics such as histograms and n_distinct, assigns I/O and CPU costs, and chooses the cheapest tree. Everything rests on [[o:one assumption]]—that the cardinality estimates are accurate.\n\n**It chooses incorrectly when:**\n- statistics are stale after a bulk load ([[r:the most common case]]) → run `ANALYZE`\n- columns are correlated → the optimiser multiplies probabilities as if the columns were independent\n- it cannot estimate a predicate, such as a user-defined function, `LIKE '%x%'`, or a bind parameter whose value is unknown\n- too many tables are joined (in Postgres, beyond `join_collapse_limit`=8 it stops searching for the optimal order)\n\nThe signature symptom is a [[r:Nested Loop with a very large loops count]], indicating that the optimiser believed the outer input was small.\n\n:::deep\nJoin order matters more than join algorithm: joining `A⋈B` and then `⋈C` may produce 10 million intermediate rows, whereas joining `A⋈C` and then `⋈B` may produce only 200. The optimiser explores orders with dynamic programming, but the search space grows factorially and must be pruned.\n\n- [[g:fix the root cause first]]: run `ANALYZE`, increase `default_statistics_target` for skewed columns, and use `CREATE STATISTICS` for correlated columns\n- forcing a plan is the [[r:last resort]]: MySQL `STRAIGHT_JOIN` or optimiser hints, Oracle hints, and Postgres `pg_hint_plan` or a session-level `SET enable_nestloop=off`. A hint freezes a plan and becomes wrong when the data changes\n- MySQL before 8.0.18 [[r:had no Hash Join]], only Block Nested Loop, so a large join without the right index was extremely slow. Knowing this boundary explains why older code often replaced joins with subqueries\n- in Postgres, insufficient `work_mem` makes a Hash Join partition into batches on disk; remember that `work_mem` applies to *each sort/hash node*, not to the query as a whole\n- a semi-join (`EXISTS`) is usually cheaper than an `IN (subquery)` returning many rows and always cheaper than applying `DISTINCT` after a join\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q4","q":"How do you trace a plan regression when a previously fast query suddenly becomes slow?","a":"The code and indexes are unchanged, yet p99 jumps from 20 ms to 4 s. There are four common causes, in descending frequency:\n\n**1. Statistics changed** — the table just received 10 million new rows and autovacuum has not run `ANALYZE` yet, so cardinality estimates drift and the plan changes. Fix it with `ANALYZE`, then tune `autovacuum_analyze_scale_factor` for large tables.\n\n**2. Parameter sniffing** — the database caches a plan using the *first* parameter value. `WHERE status = 'PENDING'` (0.1% of rows) produces an Index Scan; a later execution with `'COMPLETED'` (95% of rows) reuses that plan and performs an [[r:index scan over 9.5 million rows]].\n\nThis is especially painful in SQL Server and Oracle; Postgres has custom versus generic plans (`plan_cache_mode`).\n\n**3. Data crossed a threshold** — the table grew until its index no longer fits in the buffer pool, making every lookup perform real I/O. Nothing visibly \"changed\"; the workload simply crossed a capacity boundary.\n\n**4. Heap or index bloat** — frequent UPDATE/DELETE activity outpaces autovacuum, filling the heap and indexes with dead tuples so more pages must be read for the same rows.\n\n:::deep\nA disciplined investigation looks like this:\n\n1. use `pg_stat_statements` to determine when the query's `mean_exec_time` changed and narrow the time window\n2. compare the current plan with the stored baseline (18.1 step 6)\n3. inspect `last_analyze`, `n_dead_tup`, and `n_mod_since_analyze` in `pg_stat_user_tables`\n4. examine the buffer hit ratio and table size over time\n\nPrevention:\n- [[g:run `ANALYZE` immediately after every bulk load or migration]]; one line in a script prevents many sleepless nights\n- monitor `n_dead_tup` and tune autovacuum for write-heavy tables (`autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor` defaults to 0.2, which is far too high for a 100-million-row table)\n- on Postgres 12+, use `plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan` for queries with heavily skewed parameter distributions\n- keep plan baselines in the repository for critical queries and compare them in CI or a scheduled job\n- Oracle provides [[o:SQL Plan Management]], where only approved baselines may be used; Postgres and MySQL have no direct equivalent, so monitoring must be built around them\n\nMentioning *parameter sniffing* and *autovacuum not having reached ANALYZE yet* signals real operational experience rather than documentation knowledge.\n**The two queries that answer \"what changed?\":**\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">-- 1. Which statement regressed, and by how much</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> queryid, calls, mean_exec_time, stddev_exec_time\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> pg_stat_statements\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> query <span class=\"k\">LIKE</span> <span class=\"s\">'%FROM bookings%'</span>\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER BY</span> mean_exec_time <span class=\"k\">DESC</span> <span class=\"k\">LIMIT</span> <span class=\"n\">5</span>;\n<span class=\"c\">--</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- 2. Is it stale stats or bloat? (the usual answer)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> relname, n_live_tup, n_dead_tup,\n       n_mod_since_analyze, last_analyze, last_autovacuum\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> pg_stat_user_tables <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> relname = <span class=\"s\">'bookings'</span>;\n<span class=\"c\">--   n_mod_since_analyze = 9.4M on a 12M-row table</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--   last_analyze = 6 days ago  -&gt; the estimates are fiction</span></code></pre>\n\nA high `stddev_exec_time` with a normal `mean` is the fingerprint of [[r:parameter sniffing]]: the same statement is fast for one bind value and slow for another. A uniformly high `mean` points to stats or bloat instead.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q5","difficulty":"advanced","q":"What is a parameter-sensitive plan, and how do you diagnose a cached plan that is fast for one value but slow for another?","a":"Parameterized SQL improves safety and can reduce parse/plan work, but one reusable plan may be poor when tenant sizes or predicate values are highly skewed.\n\nTypical signatures are “fast after one restart, slow after another,” a seek suitable for a rare value being reused for a common value, or an optional `WHERE (:p IS NULL OR col = :p)` predicate forcing a compromise.\n\n**Diagnose with evidence:** capture the normalized statement and representative parameter buckets (rare/common, small/large tenant, null/non-null). For each bucket compare actual rows, estimates, loops, buffers/I/O, memory spill, duration, and plan identity.\n\nCheck histogram/statistics freshness and the engine's plan cache or Query Store. Do not clear a shared production plan cache as an experiment—it destroys evidence and perturbs unrelated workloads.\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">-- PostgreSQL: inspect the plan selected for actual values</span>\nPREPARE find_orders(bigint, text) <span class=\"k\">AS</span>\n  <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> tenant_id = $1 <span class=\"k\">AND</span> status = $2;\nEXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) EXECUTE find_orders(42, 'OPEN');</code></pre>\n\nPostgreSQL can choose custom plans based on values or a reusable generic plan; `EXPLAIN EXECUTE`, `EXPLAIN (GENERIC_PLAN)`, and `plan_cache_mode` help diagnose that choice. Overrides such as `force_custom_plan` are diagnostic or workload-specific controls, not global tuning defaults.\n\nSQL Server 2022+ at compatibility level 160 can use Parameter Sensitive Plan optimization to keep variants for eligible skewed equality predicates; inspect Query Store and dispatcher/query variants before adding hints.\n\n:::deep\n**Fix the cause in this order:**\n\n1. validate statistics and data distribution\n2. make predicates sargable and types consistent\n3. improve indexes/data model\n4. split materially different query shapes\n5. consider engine-specific adaptive-plan features, selective recompilation/custom planning, or carefully tested hints\n\nEach remedy trades execution quality against planning CPU, plan-cache memory, operational complexity, or code paths.\n\nBuild a regression workload with several selectivity buckets and production-like concurrency. A single “representative parameter” benchmark merely selects which users will see the bad plan.\n\nThis extends the plan-regression workflow in (18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q4).\n:::"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q6","difficulty":"core","q":"Where do EXPLAIN's shared hit and read numbers come from — PostgreSQL's buffer cache","a":"PostgreSQL never reads a table file directly. Every page a query touches is first copied into a fixed array of 8 KB buffers in shared memory — `shared_buffers` — and `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` is a report about exactly that array.\n\n<figure class='dgm'><svg viewBox='0 0 680 276' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='IBM Plex Mono, monospace' font-size='11' text-anchor='middle'>\n<rect x='14' y='104' width='128' height='52' rx='7' fill='#E7EAF6' stroke='#3D4E8F'/><text x='78' y='134' fill='#3D4E8F' font-weight='600'>Backend</text>\n<rect x='196' y='92' width='224' height='76' rx='7' fill='#E4F1EB' stroke='#0B6E4F'/><text x='308' y='127' fill='#085239' font-weight='600'>shared_buffers</text><text x='308' y='141' fill='#085239' font-size='9.5'>8 KB pages · clock sweep</text>\n<rect x='470' y='26' width='196' height='52' rx='7' fill='#F7F9FC' stroke='#42536C'/><text x='568' y='56' fill='#14233A' font-weight='600'>OS page cache</text>\n<rect x='470' y='186' width='196' height='52' rx='7' fill='#FFFBF2' stroke='#9A7A41'/><text x='568' y='209' fill='#9A7A41' font-weight='600'>Data files</text><text x='568' y='223' fill='#9A7A41' font-size='9.5'>heap and index</text>\n<rect x='196' y='206' width='224' height='44' rx='7' fill='#EFEAF7' stroke='#5B3E8E'/><text x='308' y='232' fill='#5B3E8E' font-weight='600'>WAL</text>\n<polyline points='142,118 192,118' fill='none' stroke='#42536C' stroke-width='1.6' marker-end='url(#pgbufa)'/>\n<circle cx='167' cy='100' r='9' fill='#14233A'/><text x='167' y='103.5' fill='#FFFFFF' font-size='9.5'>1</text>\n<text x='167' y='86' fill='#42536C' font-size='9.5'>ask for a page</text>\n<polyline points='194,146 144,146' fill='none' stroke='#0B6E4F' stroke-width='1.6' marker-end='url(#pgbufa)'/>\n<circle cx='167' cy='166' r='9' fill='#0B6E4F'/><text x='167' y='169.5' fill='#FFFFFF' font-size='9.5'>2</text>\n<text x='167' y='188' fill='#085239' font-size='9.5'>shared hit</text>\n<polyline points='420,110 446,110 446,52 468,52' fill='none' stroke='#42536C' stroke-width='1.6' marker-end='url(#pgbufa)'/>\n<circle cx='446' cy='80' r='9' fill='#14233A'/><text x='446' y='83.5' fill='#FFFFFF' font-size='9.5'>3</text>\n<text x='414' y='44' fill='#42536C' font-size='9.5'>shared read</text>\n<polyline points='568,78 568,182' fill='none' stroke='#42536C' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-dasharray='5 4' marker-end='url(#pgbufa)'/>\n<circle cx='568' cy='130' r='9' fill='#14233A'/><text x='568' y='133.5' fill='#FFFFFF' font-size='9.5'>4</text>\n<text x='614' y='134' fill='#42536C' font-size='9.5'>cold read</text>\n<polyline points='308,168 308,202' fill='none' stroke='#5B3E8E' stroke-width='1.6' marker-end='url(#pgbufp)'/>\n<circle cx='308' cy='186' r='9' fill='#5B3E8E'/><text x='308' y='189.5' fill='#FFFFFF' font-size='9.5'>5</text>\n<text x='392' y='188' fill='#5B3E8E' font-size='9.5'>WAL first, at commit</text>\n<polyline points='420,150 446,150 446,204 468,204' fill='none' stroke='#AE4536' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-dasharray='5 4' marker-end='url(#pgbufr)'/>\n<circle cx='446' cy='178' r='9' fill='#AE4536'/><text x='446' y='181.5' fill='#FFFFFF' font-size='9.5'>6</text>\n<text x='568' y='258' fill='#AE4536' font-size='9.5'>checkpointer / bgwriter</text>\n<text x='340' y='268' fill='#42536C' font-size='10' text-anchor='middle'>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) counts exactly these arrows</text>\n<defs><marker id='pgbufa' markerWidth='9' markerHeight='9' refX='7' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 z' fill='#42536C'/></marker><marker id='pgbufp' markerWidth='9' markerHeight='9' refX='7' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 z' fill='#5B3E8E'/></marker><marker id='pgbufr' markerWidth='9' markerHeight='9' refX='7' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 z' fill='#AE4536'/></marker></defs>\n</svg></figure>\n\n**The four counters, and what each one really tells you:**\n\n- `shared hit=N` — the page was already in the array, so nothing left the process.\n- `shared read=N` — it was not, so the page came from the OS page cache or from the disk. [[r:A read is not proof of disk I/O]]: PostgreSQL cannot tell the two apart, which is the double-buffering blind spot. `track_io_timing` is what turns that guess into a number.\n- `shared dirtied=N` — this query was the first to modify pages that were clean.\n- `shared written=N` — this backend had to write dirty pages out itself to make room. That is the checkpointer and the bgwriter falling behind, not a property of the query (18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q7).\n\n**Eviction is a clock sweep, not an LRU list.** Every buffer carries a usage counter that rises when the buffer is pinned and stops at 5. The sweep hand moves around the array: a buffer with a nonzero count loses one and survives, an unpinned buffer at zero is taken.\n\nA page therefore has to be ignored for several full passes before it leaves, which is why [[g:frequency beats recency]] here — one scan touching a page once does not promote it over a page read all day.\n\n**A large sequential scan cannot flush the cache.** Once a table is bigger than a quarter of `shared_buffers`, the scan runs inside a 256 KB ring of buffers and recycles them instead of sweeping the whole array, so a nightly report does not evict the working set.\n\n- Bulk writes — `COPY`, `CREATE TABLE AS` — use a 16 MB ring, capped at an eighth of `shared_buffers`.\n- `VACUUM` and `ANALYZE` use `vacuum_buffer_usage_limit`, 2 MB by default.\n\n**Read what is in the cache, not a hit ratio.** `pg_buffercache` shows which relation owns each buffer and its usage count, and `pg_statio_user_tables` gives hits and reads per table.\n\nA 99% global ratio hides the one table that misses, exactly as a global cache hit rate does (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q7).\n\n:::deep\n- `BUFFERS` is included automatically with `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` in PostgreSQL 18, so a plan read today carries the counters whether or not they were asked for; on older servers you still spell out `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)`.\n- reads are asynchronous in 18: `io_method` defaults to `worker` with three `io_workers` — `io_uring` is available on Linux builds — and `effective_io_concurrency` now defaults to 16, so a bitmap heap scan really does issue concurrent reads.\n- temp and local blocks are different memory. A temporary table lives in `temp_buffers` (8 MB per session) while sorts and hashes spill through `work_mem` (4 MB), so `temp read/written` in a plan means spilling, not cache pressure (18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q2).\n- heap pages and index pages compete for the same buffers, which is part of the price of an index nobody queries: every insert still dirties its pages and those pages still take space in the array.\n:::"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q7","difficulty":"advanced","q":"Sizing shared_buffers, and the write side: checkpoints, bgwriter and full-page writes","a":"The default `shared_buffers` is 128 MB — a value picked so the server starts anywhere, not a recommendation. The usual first move is about a quarter of RAM, and that is a starting point rather than a target, because two forces push back.\n\n- [[b:Double buffering]]: whatever is not in `shared_buffers` is usually still in the OS page cache, so enlarging the array can simply buy a second copy of the same pages.\n- [[b:Checkpoint size]]: every dirty buffer has to be written eventually, so a bigger array means a bigger burst of writes per checkpoint.\n- The number is settled by measurement: do `shared read`, backend writes in `pg_stat_io` and checkpoint write time actually fall when you raise it?\n\n**`effective_cache_size` allocates nothing.** It defaults to 4 GB and exists only for the planner: it states how much cache a query may assume, which moves the choice between an index scan and a sequential scan.\n\nSetting it to the memory the machine really leaves for PostgreSQL costs nothing and changes plans (18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q3).\n\n**The write path is where a buffer cache becomes an incident.**\n\n- A commit does not write data pages. It writes WAL and fsyncs that (`synchronous_commit = on`), while the page itself stays dirty in the array.\n- The checkpointer writes those pages later: every `checkpoint_timeout` (5 min) or when `max_wal_size` (1 GB) fills, spread across `checkpoint_completion_target` (0.9) of the interval.\n- The bgwriter trickles out victims ahead of the sweep — `bgwriter_delay` 200 ms, at most `bgwriter_lru_maxpages` (100) buffers per round — so that backends do not have to write their own.\n- `pg_stat_checkpointer` answers the first question: `num_requested` rising against `num_timed` means checkpoints are driven by WAL volume, not by the clock, and `max_wal_size` is what to raise before touching anything else.\n\n**Frequent checkpoints multiply WAL, not just I/O.** With `full_page_writes = on`, the first write to a page after each checkpoint copies the entire 8 KB page into WAL — the same mechanism that makes random-UUID inserts degrade (05-db-core-index-lock.indexes-what-they-really-are.q7).\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">-- which relations own the cache right now, and how much of it is hot</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- (approximate: relfilenode does not match for mapped catalog relations)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> c.relname,\n       count(*) * <span class=\"n\">8</span> / <span class=\"n\">1024</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> mb,\n       round(<span class=\"n\">100.0</span> * count(*) <span class=\"k\">FILTER</span> (<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> b.usagecount &gt;= <span class=\"n\">3</span>) / count(*), <span class=\"n\">1</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> pct_hot\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> pg_buffercache b <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> pg_class c <span class=\"k\">ON</span> c.relfilenode = b.relfilenode\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP BY</span> <span class=\"n\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">ORDER BY</span> <span class=\"n\">2</span> <span class=\"k\">DESC</span> <span class=\"k\">LIMIT</span> <span class=\"n\">10</span>;\n<span class=\"c\">--</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- clock-driven checkpoints, or WAL-driven ones?</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> num_timed, num_requested <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> pg_stat_checkpointer;</code></pre>\n\n:::deep\n- `huge_pages` defaults to `try`, which means it silently does nothing when the kernel has none reserved. With a large `shared_buffers` the per-backend page tables are expensive, so read `postgres -C shared_memory_size_in_huge_pages` and set `vm.nr_hugepages` to that.\n- after a restart or a failover the array is empty, so every read misses and p99 stays bad until it refills. `pg_prewarm` with the `autoprewarm` worker dumps the buffer list and reloads it at startup — the same event as a cold cache after a deploy (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q7).\n- `pg_stat_io` separates who did the writing: `backend_type` together with `context` (normal, vacuum, bulkread, bulkwrite) tells you whether pages left through the checkpointer, the bgwriter, or backends stalling to make room.\n- by tier: through T1–T3 (up to 1M req/day, ~250 rps peak) tuning `shared_buffers`, `effective_cache_size` and the checkpoint settings is `worth it`; at T4 (~25,000 rps peak) the buffer cache `needs its own scaling story` — replicas, partitioning and a cache in front of it.\n:::"}]},{"title":"Rewriting the query & reshaping the model","items":[{"id":"18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q1","q":"How should WHERE predicates be written so that indexes can actually be used?","a":"[[b:Sargable]] describes a predicate the optimizer can map to an efficient search argument for an available access method.\n\nFor an ordinary B-tree column index, comparing the indexed column directly with compatible constants/ranges is the most portable shape—but “the left side must always be a bare column” is not a law.\n\nExpression/generated-column indexes, partial indexes, bitmap/index-merge plans and other access methods change what is indexable.\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">-- Usually blocks a plain index on created_at because every row's value is transformed.</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"f\">DATE</span>(created_at) = <span class=\"s\">'2026-07-31'</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- Portable range form for a half-open day.</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> created_at &gt;= <span class=\"s\">'2026-07-31'</span>\n  <span class=\"k\">AND</span> created_at &lt;  <span class=\"s\">'2026-08-01'</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- Expression index can make the matching expression searchable.</span>\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE INDEX</span> users_lower_email_idx <span class=\"k\">ON</span> users ((<span class=\"f\">lower</span>(email)));\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"f\">lower</span>(email) = <span class=\"s\">'a@b.vn'</span></code></pre>\n\nCommon obstacles are a transformation without a matching expression/generated-column index, an implicit conversion applied to the indexed side, an incompatible collation/operator, and a leading-wildcard search unsupported by a plain B-tree.\n\nRewrite date/time predicates with correct timezone and half-open boundaries; match bind-parameter types; use full-text/trigram/search-specific indexes when substring semantics require them.\n\n:::deep\n`!=`, `NOT IN`, `OR`, and low-cardinality columns are not automatically “non-indexable.” Their selectivity and cost often make a scan cheaper, while some engines can use multiple ranges, bitmap/index-merge operations, partial/composite indexes or histograms.\n\n`OR` → `UNION ALL` is safe only when duplicate semantics and branch overlap are handled; measure the actual plan.\n\n`NOT IN` with a nullable subquery has a separate three-valued-logic trap; `NOT EXISTS` often expresses the intended anti-join, but plan quality remains workload-dependent. Expression indexes add write/storage cost and require the query expression to match engine rules.\n\nMySQL 8.4 can use indexed generated expressions; PostgreSQL supports indexes on expressions.\n\nSargability only creates an access-path option. The cost-based optimizer may correctly choose a scan when many rows qualify or the table is small. Compare estimates, actual rows, buffers/I/O and timing on representative data (18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q2).\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q2","q":"How should COUNT(*) and pagination be handled on a table with tens of millions of rows?","a":"These are the two most expensive features on a list screen, and neither usually [[o:needs to be exact]].\n\n**Total COUNT(*)** — both MySQL/InnoDB and Postgres must scan because under MVCC every transaction may see a different row count, so the result cannot be cached globally.\n\n- use an [[g:estimate]] for display-only counts: `SELECT reltuples::bigint FROM pg_class WHERE relname='bookings'` is immediate and usually within a few percent\n- use a [[g:capped count]]: `SELECT count(*) FROM (SELECT 1 FROM t WHERE ... LIMIT 1001) x` lets the UI display \"1000+\"\n- maintain a [[g:counter table]] through a trigger or CDC when exactness is required\n- [[r:remove the total page count entirely]]; Google, Slack, and Stripe expose only next/previous navigation\n\n**Pagination** — `OFFSET 500000 LIMIT 20` must read and discard 500,000 rows.\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">-- Keyset: fast and stable, using the (created_at, id) index</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> id, code, created_at <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> bookings\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> user_id = ?\n  <span class=\"k\">AND</span> (created_at, id) &lt; (?, ?)   <span class=\"c\">-- cursor from the previous page</span>\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER BY</span> created_at <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>, id <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>\n<span class=\"k\">LIMIT</span> <span class=\"n\">20</span>;</code></pre>\n\nThe `id` tie-breaker is [[o:mandatory]]. Without it, rows sharing the same `created_at` value will be skipped or repeated between pages.\n\n:::deep\n- the [[r:limitation of keyset pagination]] is that it cannot jump directly to \"page 500\". If the business genuinely requires that, retain `OFFSET` but enforce a hard maximum, or use Elasticsearch `search_after`\n- cursors returned to clients should be [[o:opaque]] (a base64-encoded tuple plus a signature); exposing raw `created_at` leaks information and lets clients forge cursors\n- ORDER BY [[g:must match the index order]], including ASC/DESC direction, to eliminate sorting. Postgres can scan an index backwards, but a multicolumn index with mixed directions (`a ASC, b DESC`) must be declared correctly when created\n- a covering index (`INCLUDE` in Postgres, or extra indexed columns in MySQL) enables an [[g:index-only scan]] that avoids the heap and is often many times faster\n- `COUNT(*)` and `COUNT(1)` have [[o:identical performance]]; the difference is an old myth. `COUNT(col)` differs because it excludes NULL values\n- for deep pagination during export, use a database cursor or an asynchronous job (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q3) rather than making the client iterate with OFFSET\n\nAn OTA customer's booking history is naturally ordered by descending time, making keyset pagination the natural choice and eliminating the need for a total page count.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q3","q":"For heavy aggregation, should you pre-aggregate, use a materialized view, or move the workload to OLAP?","a":"A \"12-month revenue by airline\" dashboard scans 200 million rows every time it opens. No index can rescue that workload; you [[o:must change how the result is computed, not how it is looked up]].\n\nThe options, from least to most expensive, are:\n\n**1. Cache the result** — use Redis with a TTL of 5–60 minutes. It is the cheapest option and should be tried first. It is enough for most dashboards.\n\n**2. Materialized view** — use Postgres `CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW` and refresh it periodically:\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"k\">CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW</span> mv_revenue_daily <span class=\"k\">AS</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> airline_id, created_at::date <span class=\"k\">AS</span> d,\n       <span class=\"f\">count</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> bookings, <span class=\"f\">sum</span>(total) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> bookings <span class=\"k\">GROUP BY</span> <span class=\"n\">1</span>, <span class=\"n\">2</span>;\n<span class=\"c\">--</span>\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE UNIQUE INDEX</span> <span class=\"k\">ON</span> mv_revenue_daily(airline_id, d);\n<span class=\"k\">REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</span> <span class=\"k\">CONCURRENTLY</span> mv_revenue_daily;</code></pre>\n\n`CONCURRENTLY` requires a unique index but [[g:does not block readers]]. Without it, the entire view is locked during refresh.\n\n**3. Incremental rollup table** — update a `revenue_daily` table through a job or CDC only for days that changed. This is cheaper than a full refresh and can retain years of data.\n\n**4. Move to OLAP** — feed ClickHouse, BigQuery, or Redshift through CDC such as Debezium when arbitrary ad-hoc queries over billions of rows are required.\n\n:::deep\nThe decision boundary is: [[o:is the query known in advance?]]\n\n- known queries with few dimensions → use a rollup table or materialized view\n- ad-hoc queries with many user-selected dimensions → use a columnar OLAP system. Forcing ad-hoc analytics into OLTP is [[r:one of the surest ways to take production down]]\n\n- materialized views [[r:do not update themselves]] in Postgres (Oracle supports fast refresh on commit); they need a scheduled job, and that job needs monitoring\n- incremental rollups must handle [[o:late-arriving data]], such as a booking amended three days later; refresh a rolling window of the most recent N days, not only today\n- a read replica is a useful intermediate step: move all reporting to the replica and accept a few seconds of lag. It is often sufficient and costs little engineering effort\n- columnar OLAP is fast because it reads only required columns, compresses by column, and vectorises execution; the trade-off is poor point-lookup and individual-update performance\n- use [[g:approximation]] when allowed: `HyperLogLog` (`APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT`) estimates unique visitors with roughly 2% error and can be hundreds of times faster\n- a window function such as `SUM() OVER` often replaces a self-join or correlated subquery, staying within OLTP while running much faster\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q4","q":"How do you optimize the application layer through connection pools, statement timeouts, batching, and fetch size?","a":"A well-optimized query can still be slow because much of the elapsed time may not be inside the database.\n\n**Connection pool (HikariCP)**\n- a reasonable pool size is [[o:cores × 2 + number of spindles]], usually 10–20. [[r:A pool of 200 is not faster]]; it merely moves the queue from the application into the database and makes every query uniformly slow.\n- `connectionTimeout`, the time spent waiting for a connection, should be [[g:short]] (2–5 s) so the service fails fast instead of building a backlog\n- an exhausted pool is usually a [[o:symptom]], not the cause: a slow query may hold a connection too long, or a transaction may remain open around an outbound HTTP call\n\n**Timeouts — three layers, ordered from shortest to longest**\n- database `statement_timeout` (for example, 5 s) is the final safety net and should [[g:always be configured]]\n- application `@Transactional(timeout=...)`\n- client socket/HTTP timeout\n\nWithout the database layer, a broken query can run for 40 minutes while holding locks for the entire duration.\n\n**Batching and fetching**\n- writes: `rewriteBatchedStatements=true` on MySQL plus `hibernate.jdbc.batch_size=50` turns 1,000 individual INSERTs into roughly 20 round trips\n- large reads: configure `fetchSize` (Postgres also requires `autoCommit=false`) to stream results rather than loading the entire result set into the heap, preventing OOM\n\n**Derive the pool size instead of guessing it.** Little's Law gives the number directly: concurrency = throughput × service time.\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">// a service at 250 rps peak, average 8 ms inside the database</span>\n<span class=\"c\">//   250 x 0.008 = 2 connections busy on average</span>\n<span class=\"c\">//   x3 for variance and slow outliers -> a pool of 6-10 is right</span>\n<span class=\"c\">//</span>\n<span class=\"c\">// a pool of 200 for the same load does not add capacity: the database still</span>\n<span class=\"c\">// executes ~2 at a time, and the other 198 requests wait *inside the database*</span>\n<span class=\"c\">// where no application metric can see them.</span></code></pre>\n\n**One configuration, all three timeout layers, ordered shortest to longest.** Order is the whole point: an inner layer that outlives an outer one can never fire.\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\"># application.yml</span>\nspring:\n  datasource:\n    hikari:\n      maximum-pool-size: <span class=\"n\">10</span>          <span class=\"c\"># from the arithmetic above, not from a habit</span>\n      minimum-idle: <span class=\"n\">10</span>               <span class=\"c\"># same as max: a fixed pool has no warm-up cliff</span>\n      connection-timeout: <span class=\"n\">3000</span>      <span class=\"c\"># wait for a connection, then fail fast (3s)</span>\n      validation-timeout: <span class=\"n\">1000</span>\n      max-lifetime: <span class=\"n\">1740000</span>         <span class=\"c\"># 29m — must be under the DB/proxy idle cut</span>\n      keepalive-time: <span class=\"n\">120000</span>\n      leak-detection-threshold: <span class=\"n\">20000</span>  <span class=\"c\"># names the code path that held it</span>\n  jpa:\n    open-in-view: <span class=\"k\">false</span>            <span class=\"c\"># or a connection is held for the whole render</span>\n    properties:\n      hibernate.jdbc.batch_size: <span class=\"n\">50</span>\n      hibernate.order_inserts: <span class=\"k\">true</span>   <span class=\"c\"># batching only groups adjacent same-table statements</span>\n      hibernate.order_updates: <span class=\"k\">true</span>\n      hibernate.jdbc.fetch_size: <span class=\"n\">200</span>\n      jakarta.persistence.query.timeout: <span class=\"n\">4000</span>   <span class=\"c\"># 2. per statement (4s)</span>\n<span class=\"c\">#</span>\n<span class=\"c\"># 1. innermost — the database's own net, and the only one a runaway query obeys</span>\n<span class=\"c\">#    Postgres: statement_timeout = 5s   ·   MySQL: max_execution_time = 5000</span>\n<span class=\"c\"># 3. outermost — the HTTP client calling this service, longer than both</span></code></pre>\n\nTwo things silently switch batching back off, and neither reports anything:\n\n- an `IDENTITY` generator disables insert batching entirely, because Hibernate has to read each generated key back before it can continue. Use a sequence or a table generator wherever insert throughput matters.\n- a flush in the middle of the batch — a query against the same table, or `saveAndFlush` in a loop — closes the batch early and you get the round trips back.\n\n:::warn Batching that reports success and does nothing\nOn MySQL, `hibernate.jdbc.batch_size` alone changes almost nothing: without `rewriteBatchedStatements=true` on the JDBC URL, the driver still sends one statement per round trip. Nothing warns you — the only signal is the round-trip count.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- [[r:long transactions]] are the primary enemy: open a transaction, call a partner API for 3 s, then commit. The connection is held uselessly for 3 s, MVCC retains an old snapshot, and autovacuum cannot reclaim dead tuples. Move external calls outside the transaction.\n- Spring Boot's `open-in-view` is [[r:enabled]] by default; it holds a connection while rendering the view and conceals N+1 queries. Disable it with `spring.jpa.open-in-view=false`, then repair the failures it reveals because those paths were already incorrect.\n- detect N+1 (06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q6) with `hibernate.generate_statistics` or datasource-proxy; fix it with `JOIN FETCH`, `@EntityGraph`, or `@BatchSize`, not `EAGER`\n- use [[o:separate pools by workload]]: a small reporting pool and a primary customer-request pool prevent a heavy job from exhausting connections needed by the money path\n- `PreparedStatement` improves both security and performance by avoiding repeated parsing, but an oversized prepared-statement cache consumes database memory\n- measure [[g:pool acquisition time]] separately from query execution time; Micrometer already exposes `hikaricp.connections.acquire`. Without that metric, diagnosis is guesswork\n:::","difficulty":"extra"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q5","q":"A job must insert two million rows and process a work queue. What breaks if you treat it like ordinary application writes?","a":"Bulk work fails differently from request-scoped work. One transaction around two million rows holds locks and an MVCC snapshot for its entire runtime, blocks vacuum, and loses everything if it fails at 98%.\n\n**Structure the job so failure is cheap:**\n\n- process in [[g:chunks]] with a commit per chunk, plus a checkpoint recording the last id done — a restart resumes rather than repeats\n- iterate with [[g:keyset pagination]]; `OFFSET 1900000` re-reads everything before it\n- clear the persistence context each chunk, or Hibernate keeps every entity of the run in memory\n- for the initial load, a bulk path (`COPY`, `LOAD DATA`) beats even batched inserts by an order of magnitude\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">-- queue table: many workers, no contention, no double processing</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> id, payload <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> jobs\n <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> status = <span class=\"s\">'PENDING'</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> run_after &lt;= now()\n <span class=\"k\">ORDER BY</span> run_after\n <span class=\"k\">LIMIT</span> <span class=\"n\">100</span>\n <span class=\"k\">FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</span>;                <span class=\"c\">-- locked rows are skipped, not waited on</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- chunked scan: resume from the last id, never OFFSET</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> id &gt; :lastId <span class=\"k\">ORDER BY</span> id <span class=\"k\">LIMIT</span> <span class=\"n\">1000</span>;</code></pre>\n\n`FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` is what makes a database usable as a work queue: each worker takes rows nobody else holds, so N workers scale linearly without a broker (25-microservice.03-distributed-data-consistency.q2).\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Cost people miss</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Bulk `UPDATE`/`DELETE` via JPQL</td><td>bypasses the persistence context — stale entities remain in memory</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mass `DELETE`</td><td>leaves dead tuples and bloat; partition-drop is often the right tool</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Insert into an indexed table</td><td>every index is written too; dropping and rebuilding can be faster for a big load</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Long-running read</td><td>holds a snapshot and blocks cleanup for the whole run</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n:::warn A batch job competes with live traffic\nIt shares the pool, the CPU and the buffer cache. Give it its own small pool, run it off-peak, and throttle it — a \"quick backfill\" is a classic cause of a production latency spike (18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q4).\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- make the job [[o:idempotent per chunk]]: it will be re-run. A unique constraint or an upsert keyed on the natural identifier turns a rerun into a no-op (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q1).\n- keep chunk size tunable and measure it. Too small pays per-transaction overhead, too large brings back the long-transaction problems you were avoiding (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q2).\n- for the queue table, index on `(status, run_after)` and keep the table narrow; a queue that grows forever slows down every claim, so archive completed rows.\n- add attempt counting and a dead-letter status to the queue rows, otherwise one poison job is retried until someone notices.\n- report progress: rows done, rate, estimated finish. A silent job that has run for six hours cannot be judged as stuck or merely slow.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q6","difficulty":"advanced","q":"When should a slow query become a cache, materialized view, CDC projection, or OLAP model?","a":"Choose the read shape from measured frequency, latency target, result size, freshness budget, write rate, and rebuild cost. The options are different contracts:\n\n- a cache is disposable and fast, but invalidation, eviction, stampede control, and stale reads are part of the design\n- a materialized view is a database-managed or scheduled snapshot; define refresh blocking, versioning, and failure behavior\n- a CDC projection is a separately stored read model with a watermark, replay, schema/delete handling, and repair path\n- an OLAP model is for scans and aggregates over governed history; it needs late-data, correction, lineage, and access-control rules\n\nDo not use any of these to hide an unmeasured query. First capture the plan, cardinality, selectivity, rows examined, CPU/I/O, concurrency, and write amplification. Then compare the new path against the authoritative query using a representative data distribution.\n\n**Refresh/rebuild gate:** record the source snapshot and change position, build a new version, catch it up, compare counts/checksums and semantic invariants, observe freshness and error rate, and only then swap or route traffic.\n\nIf a correction or delete cannot be replayed, the “read model” is not safely rebuildable.\n\nA dual-write from request code is usually the least defensible option because it couples two commits without a shared recovery point.\n\nPrefer a transactionally coupled outbox or a database-log/CDC path, then make the projection idempotent (06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q7), (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2).\n\n:::deep\nA read model is justified when its freshness and operational cost are explicit. If a detail page needs the newest authoritative value, read the authority; let the projection serve browse, search, reports, or aggregates whose stale window is acceptable.\n:::"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q7","difficulty":"core","q":"INNER vs LEFT JOIN — what does each return, and why does a filter in `WHERE` quietly turn a LEFT JOIN into an INNER one?","a":"- [[g:INNER JOIN]] keeps only the pairs that match the `ON` condition.\n- [[g:LEFT JOIN]] keeps every left row; where nothing matches, the right side's columns come back `NULL`.\n\nThe trap is where the filter goes:\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">-- every customer, with their PAID orders if any</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> c.id, o.id\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> customer c\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">ON</span> o.customer_id = c.id <span class=\"k\">AND</span> o.status = <span class=\"s\">'PAID'</span>;\n<span class=\"c\">──── the same filter in WHERE: customers with no paid order vanish</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> c.id, o.id\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> customer c\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">ON</span> o.customer_id = c.id\n<span class=\"r\">WHERE o.status = 'PAID'</span>;     <span class=\"c\">-- NULL = 'PAID' is unknown, so the row is dropped</span></code></pre>\n\n`ON` decides which right rows *match*; `WHERE` decides which result rows *survive*. A `WHERE` condition on the right table that a `NULL` cannot pass removes exactly the rows the `LEFT` was there to keep — and the optimizer simply plans it as an inner join.\n\n**\"Rows with no match\" — three spellings, one of them wrong:**\n<pre><code><span class=\"k\">WHERE NOT EXISTS</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"n\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.customer_id = c.id)  <span class=\"c\">-- anti-join, clearest</span>\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">ON</span> o.customer_id = c.id <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.id <span class=\"k\">IS NULL</span>       <span class=\"c\">-- same result: test the key</span>\n<span class=\"r\">WHERE c.id NOT IN (SELECT customer_id FROM orders)</span>                     <span class=\"c\">-- one NULL → zero rows</span></code></pre>\n\n:::tip The rule\nConditions about which rows match go in `ON`; conditions about which results you want go in `WHERE`. For \"has none\", write `NOT EXISTS`.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- `NOT IN` fails because `x NOT IN (1, NULL)` means `x &lt;&gt; 1 AND x &lt;&gt; NULL`, and the second half is unknown for every `x`. One nullable `customer_id` column is all it takes.\n- a LEFT JOIN to a one-to-many table multiplies the left rows: `COUNT(*)` then counts orders, and a customer with none counts as 1. Count the right key (`COUNT(o.id)`) or aggregate in a subquery first.\n- `WHERE` filters rows before grouping, `HAVING` filters groups after it. Put a condition in `WHERE` whenever it needs no aggregate, so fewer rows reach the `GROUP BY`.\n- `RIGHT JOIN` is a `LEFT JOIN` with the tables swapped, and many teams ban it for readability. `FULL OUTER JOIN` is for reconciliation — rows missing on either side; MySQL has none, so emulate it with `UNION`.\n- inner joins can be reordered freely and the optimizer does; outer joins constrain that freedom, which is one way an unnecessary `LEFT JOIN` costs a plan (18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q3).\n- PostgreSQL and MySQL 8 both plan `NOT EXISTS` as an anti-join, so choosing it over `LEFT JOIN … IS NULL` is readability, not speed — check with `EXPLAIN` before believing otherwise.\n:::"}]}]},"hasVi":true,"stem":"18-query-optimization","sourceOwners":{"10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q1":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q2":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q7":"design-review-framework","11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q12":"design-review-framework","11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q21":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framewor